- Retrieved practices:
- Avoid invalid pointer-to-member access in C++
WHAT: Using .* or ->* with a pointer-to-member that refers to a member not present in the dynamic type of the object, or using a null pointer-to-member, causes undefined behavior.
WHY: Undefined behavior can lead to memory corruption, crashes, or exploitable security vulnerabilities.
HOW: Ensure the dynamic type of the object actually defines the targeted member before using .* or ->*; never use null pointer-to-member values; avoid casting pointer-to-member values across unrelated types.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `void (Base::*pm)() = nullptr; (obj.*pm)();` After (safe): `void (Base::*pm)() = &Base::f; if (pm) (obj.*pm)();`

- Detect errors when converting a string to a number in C++ streams
WHAT: Failing to detect conversion errors from std::cin (e.g., non-numeric or out-of-range input) can leave variables uninitialized or set to unintended values while code continues execution.
WHY: Using uninitialized or incorrect values can cause undefined behavior, logic errors, or security vulnerabilities such as incorrect authorization or corrupted state.
HOW: Enable exceptions on std::cin for failbit and badbit, wrap input operations in try/catch, and treat any std::istream::failure as an error that must be handled before using the read variables.
EXAMPLE:
Before: `int i,j; std::cin >> i >> j; // i/j may be invalid or uninitialized`
After: `int i,j; std::cin.exceptions(std::istream::failbit|std::istream::badbit); try { std::cin >> i >> j; } catch (std::istream::failure&) { /* handle error */ }`

- Avoid information leakage when passing objects across trust boundaries
WHAT: Failing to fully initialize all fields (including padding and reserved fields) of objects or structs before copying them across a trust boundary can leak uninitialized memory contents.
WHY: Attackers can read sensitive kernel or process memory, potentially exposing secrets, internal state, or enabling further exploitation.
HOW: Before returning or sending any struct/class instance to less-trusted code, explicitly initialize or zero all fields (including padding/reserved areas), or use APIs/types that guarantee zero-initialization.
EXAMPLE (C++): Before: `MyStruct s; some_fields_set(s); send_to_user(s);` After: `MyStruct s{}; some_fields_set(s); secure_memset_unused_fields(s); send_to_user(s);`

- Avoid information leakage when passing C++ class objects across trust boundaries
WHAT: Copying raw class/struct objects (e.g., via memcpy or copy_to_user) can leak uninitialized padding bytes or unused bits to less-privileged code.
WHY: Attackers can infer sensitive data from leaked padding, leading to information disclosure across privilege boundaries.
HOW: Serialize only explicitly initialized fields into a packed buffer; mask off unused bits, avoid copying raw object memory, and reconstruct the object on the receiving side from the serialized representation.
EXAMPLE (C++):
// Before: may leak padding
copy_to_user(usr_buf, &obj, sizeof(obj));
// After: serialize initialized fields only
std::size_t size = sizeof(buf);
if (obj.serialize(buf, size)) copy_to_user(usr_buf, buf, used_size);

- Close files when no longer needed
WHAT: Failing to close file streams (e.g., before calling std::terminate()) can leave file descriptors and buffers in an undefined or inconsistent state.
WHY: Unclosed files can cause data loss, resource exhaustion, and undefined behavior that may be exploitable or lead to denial of service.
HOW: Explicitly call close() on file streams and check for errors before abnormal termination, or rely on RAII by limiting the scope of file objects so they are destroyed (and closed) before termination.
EXAMPLE: Before (noncompliant): std::fstream file(name); /* use file */ std::terminate(); After (compliant): std::fstream file(name); /* use file */ file.close(); if (file.fail()) { /* handle error */ } std::terminate();

- Do not leak resources when handling exceptions
WHAT: Failing to release dynamically allocated resources when an exception is thrown (for example, returning or rethrowing before delete/free) causes resource leaks.
WHY: Resource leaks can exhaust memory or other resources, leading to denial of service, instability, or degraded performance.
HOW: Use RAII (resource acquisition is initialization) objects (e.g., smart pointers, wrapper classes) so that destructors automatically release resources in all control paths, including exceptions; avoid raw new/delete in code that can throw.
EXAMPLE: Before (leaks on exception): `T* p = new T(); risky(); delete p;` After (no leak): `std::unique_ptr<T> p = std::make_unique<T>(); risky();`



